Testament.","verses":1831,"father":"Joseph","mother":"Mary","v":[2,7,10,19,22,23,24]},{"id":"timotheus_2863","name":"Timotheus","also":"Timothy","summary":"Honouring God, a young disciple who was Paul’s companion in many of his journeyings. His mother, Eunice, and his grandmother, Lois, are mentioned as eminent for their piety (2 Tim. 1:5).","verses":24,"father":"","mother":"Eunice","v":[10]},{"id":"apollos_276","name":"Apollos","also":"","summary":"A Jew “born at Alexandria,” a man well versed in the Scriptures and eloquent (Acts 18:24; R.V., “learned”). He came to Ephesus (about A.D. 49), where he spake “boldly” in the synagogue (18:26), although he did not know as yet that Jesus of Nazareth was the Messiah.","verses":10,"father":"","mother":"","v":[12]},{"id":"stephanas_2801","name":"Stephanas","also":"","summary":"Crown, a member of the church at Corinth, whose family were among those the apostle had baptized (1 Cor. 1:16; 16:15, 17). He has been supposed by some to have been the “jailer of Philippi” (comp. Acts 16:33).","verses":3,"father":"","mother":"","v":[15,17]},{"id":"achaicus_60","name":"Achaicus","also":"","summary":"(1 Cor. 16:17), one of the members of the church of Corinth who, with Fortunatus and Stephanas, visited Paul while he was at Ephesus, for the purpose of consulting him on the affairs of the church.","verses":1,"father":"","mother":"","v":[17]},{"id":"fortunatus_1258","name":"Fortunatus","also":"","summary":"Fortunate, a disciple of Corinth who visited Paul at Ephesus, and returned with Stephanas and Achaicus, the bearers of the apostle’s first letter to the Corinthians (1 Cor. 16:17).","verses":1,"father":"","mother":"","v":[17]},{"id":"aquila_279","name":"Aquila","also":"","summary":"Eagle, a native of Pontus, by occupation a tent-maker, whom Paul met on his first visit to Corinth (Acts 18:2). Along with his wife Priscilla he had fled from Rome in consequence of a decree (A.D. 50) by Claudius commanding all Jews to leave the city.","verses":6,"father":"","mother":"","v":[19]},{"id":"priscilla_2370","name":"Priscilla","also":"Prisca","summary":"The wife of Aquila (Acts 18:2), who is never mentioned without her. Her name sometimes takes the precedence of his (Rom. 16:3; 2 Tim. 4:19).","verses":6,"father":"","mother":"","v":[19]},{"id":"paul_2479","name":"Paul","also":"Saul, Mercurius","summary":"Saul was born about the same time as our Lord. His circumcision-name was Saul, and probably the name Paul was also given to him in infancy “for use in the Gentile world,” as “Saul” would be his Hebrew home-name.","verses":179,"father":"","mother":"","v":[21]}],"places":[{"id":"galatia_431","name":"Galatia","kind":"Region","lat":37.5,"lon":32.5,"summary":"Has been called the “Gallia” of the East, Roman writers calling its inhabitants Galli. They were an intermixture of Gauls and Greeks, and hence were called Gallo-Graeci, and the country Gallo-Graecia.","verses":6,"v":[1]},{"id":"jerusalem_636","name":"Jerusalem","kind":"City","lat":31.77667,"lon":35.23417,"summary":"Called also Salem, Ariel, Jebus, the “city of God,” the “holy city;” by the modern Arabs el-Khuds, meaning “the holy;” once “the city of Judah” (2 Chr. 25:28).","verses":754,"v":[3]},{"id":"macedonia_747","name":"Macedonia","kind":"Region","lat":40.73292,"lon":22.9352,"summary":"In New Testament times, was a Roman province lying north of Greece. It was governed by a propraetor with the title of proconsul. Paul was summoned by the vision of the “man of Macedonia” to preach the gospel there (Acts 16:9).","verses":21,"v":[5]},{"id":"ephesus_400","name":"Ephesus","kind":"City","lat":37.94194,"lon":27.33972,"summary":"The capital of proconsular Asia, which was the western part of Asia Minor. It was colonized principally from Athens. In the time of the Romans it bore the title of “the first and greatest metropolis of Asia.”","verses":17,"v":[8]},{"id":"achaia_16","name":"Achaia","kind":"Region","lat":37.5,"lon":22.5,"summary":"The name originally of a narrow strip of territory in Greece, on the north-west of the Peloponnesus. Subsequently it was applied by the Romans to the whole Peloponnesus, now called the Morea, and the south of Greece.","verses":10,"v":[15]},{"id":"asia_108","name":"Asia","kind":"Region","lat":39.64015,"lon":31.03325,"summary":"Is used to denote Proconsular Asia, a Roman province which embraced the western parts of Asia Minor, and of which Ephesus was the capital, in Acts 2:9; 6:9; 16:6; 19:10, 22; 20:4, 16, 18, etc., and probably Asia Minor in Acts 19:26, 27; 21:27; 24:18; 27:2.","verses":18,"v":[19]}],"events":[],"traditions":[["sabbath-and-lords-day",1,2],["tithing",1,2]],"sources":["Barnes","JFB","MHCC","Wesley"],"tags":{"bsb":[[1161,4012,3588,3048,1519,3588,40,4771,4160,4160,4160,5618,1299,1299,3588,1577,1053,1053],[1520,1520,1520,1520,2596,2596,4521,1538,4771,4771,5087,5087,3844,1437,1437,2137,2137,2137,2343,2343,2343,2443,2443,3752,2064,2064,3361,3048,1096,1096,1096],[1161,3854,3854,3854,3992,3992,3992,1992,1223,3778,1381,1381,667,667,4771,5485,1519,2419],[1161,1437,1510,1510,514,3588,2504,4198,4198,4198,4198,4198,4198,4862,1473],[3752,1330,1330,1330,3109,1161,2064,2064,2064,4314,4771,1063,1330,1330,1330,1330,1330,3109],[5177,3887,3887,3887,4314,4771,4771,2228,2532,3914,3914,3914,2443,2443,4771,4311,4311,1473,1473,1473,1473,3757,4198,4198],[1063,2309,2309,2309,2309,3708,3708,4771,737,1722,1722,3938,1679,1679,1961,1961,5100,5550,4314,4771,1437,3588,2962,2010],[1161,1961,1961,1961,1722,2181,2193,4005],[1063,3173,3173,2374,2532,1756,1756,455,455,1473,1473,2532,2532,4183,480,480],[1437,5095,2064,991,991,991,2443,1096,1096,870,870,870,4314,4314,4314,4314,4771,1063,2038,2038,2038,3588,2041,2962,2962,2962,5613,5613,2504,2504],[3361,5100,3767,1848,1848,1848,1848,1848,4311,846,846,846,846,1722,1515,2443,2443,2064,2064,2064,4314,1473,1063,1551,1551,1551,846,3326,3326,3588,80],[1161,4012,3588,80,625,3870,3870,3870,846,2443,2064,4314,4771,3326,3588,80,1510,1510,3756,3843,3843,2307,2443,2064,3568,1161